The best approach is recursion. Compare the corresponding nodes of both trees, checking their values and structure.

### Java solution

```

class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        // Both trees are empty
        if (p == null && q == null) {
            return true;
        }

        // Only one tree is empty
        if (p == null || q == null) {
            return false;
        }

        // Values must match, and both subtrees must match
        return p.val == q.val
            && isSameTree(p.left, q.left)
            && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}

```

### How it works

There are three cases:

1. Both nodes are `null`: Return `true` because both trees have the same structure at this position.
2. Only one node is `null`: Return `false` because the structures differ.
3. Both nodes exist: Their values must match, and their left and right subtrees must also be identical.

For example, consider:

Tree P

Tree Q

Both roots have value `1`, both left children have value `2`, and both right children have value `3`. Their structures and values match, so the answer is `true`.

### Complexity analysis

- Time: \\(O(n)\\), where \\(n\\) is the number of nodes examined. Each corresponding node is visited at most once.
- Space: \\(O(h)\\) auxiliary space for the recursion stack, where \\(h\\) is the maximum tree height. In the worst case, this is \\(O(n)\\).

Interview tip: Don't compare only the node values. Example 2 shows that even when the values are `1` and `2` in both trees, different left/right child positions make the trees different.

The simplest approach is to build the string character by character. For each character in `target`, first append `'a'`, then keep incrementing the last character until it matches the target character, adding every intermediate string to the answer.

### Java Solution

```

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<String> stringSequence(String target) {
        List<String> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < target.length(); i++) {
            sb.append('a');
            ans.add(sb.toString());

            while (sb.charAt(i) != target.charAt(i)) {
                sb.setCharAt(i, (char)(sb.charAt(i) + 1));
                ans.add(sb.toString());
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

```

### How it works

For `target = "abc"`:

| Operation                | Screen | Added to answer |
| ------------------------ | ------ | --------------- |
| Append `'a'`             | `a`    | `a`             |
| Append `'a'`             | `aa`   | `aa`            |
| Increment last character | `ab`   | `ab`            |
| Append `'a'`             | `aba`  | `aba`           |
| Increment last character | `abb`  | `abb`           |
| Increment last character | `abc`  | `abc`           |

Result: `["a", "aa", "ab", "aba", "abb", "abc"]`

### Complexity analysis

Let \\(n\\) be the length of `target` and \\(k\\) be the number of strings returned.

- Time complexity: \\(O(nk)\\) — each generated string requires copying up to \\(n\\) characters.
- Auxiliary space: \\(O(n)\\) for the `StringBuilder`, excluding the output list.

Why is this minimum? Each target character must be typed separately using Key 1, and then incremented from `'a'` to its required character using Key 2. This uses the minimum possible key presses.

A cleaner way to solve this problem is to maintain the effective index of the elements after deletions, without actually modifying the array.

## Java solution (Greedy)

```

class Solution {
    public int minDeletion(int[] nums) {
        int deletions = 0;
        int count = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {
            if (count % 2 == 1 && nums[count - 1] == num) {
                deletions++;
            } else {
                count++;
            }
        }

        if (count % 2 == 1) {
            deletions++;
        }

        return deletions;
    }
}

```

## How it works

- `count` represents the length of the array after accounting for deletions.
- If `count` is odd, the current element would form a pair with the previous kept element.
- If those two elements are equal, delete the current element.
- Otherwise, keep it and increment `count`.
- At the end, if `count` is odd, delete one more element to make the length even.

### Dry run

Input: `nums = [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3]`

| Current element | `count` before | Action                  | `count` after | Deletions |
| --------------- | -------------- | ----------------------- | ------------- | --------- |
| 1               | 0              | Keep                    | 1             | 0         |
| 1               | 1              | Delete (duplicate pair) | 1             | 1         |
| 2               | 1              | Keep                    | 2             | 1         |
| 2               | 2              | Keep                    | 3             | 1         |
| 3               | 3              | Delete (duplicate pair) | 3             | 2         |
| 3               | 3              | Delete (duplicate pair) | 3             | 3         |

The above approach has a flaw: it compares against `nums[count - 1]`, which does not reliably represent the last kept element after deletions. A safer and simpler solution is to use the index-based greedy approach below.

## Recommended solution

```

class Solution {
    public int minDeletion(int[] nums) {
        int deletions = 0;
        int i = 0;

        while (i < nums.length - 1) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i + 1]) {
                deletions++;
                i++;
            } else {
                i += 2;
            }
        }

        if ((nums.length - deletions) % 2 != 0) {
            deletions++;
        }

        return deletions;
    }
}

```

Complexity: \\(O(n)\\) time and \\(O(1)\\) extra space.

### Java Solution

```
class Solution {
    public int sumOfSquares(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int sum = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                sum += nums[i - 1] * nums[i - 1];
            }
        }

        return sum;
    }
}
```

### Explanation

- `n = nums.length` stores the array length.
- Iterate from `i = 1` to `n` because the problem uses 1-based indexing.
- If `n % i == 0`, the element at index `i - 1` is special.
- Add its square to `sum`.
- Return the final sum.

### Dry Run

Input: `nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]`

| `i` | `4 % i == 0`? | Square added   | Sum |
| --- | ------------- | -------------- | --- |
| 1   | Yes           | \\(1^2 = 1\\)  | 1   |
| 2   | Yes           | \\(2^2 = 4\\)  | 5   |
| 3   | No            | 0              | 5   |
| 4   | Yes           | \\(4^2 = 16\\) | 21  |

Output: `21`

### Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: \\(O(n)\\) — we iterate through the array indices once.
- Space Complexity: \\(O(1)\\) — we use only a few extra variables.

### Java Solution

```
class Solution {
    public int[] findErrorNums(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] count = new int[n + 1];

        for (int num : nums) {
            count[num]++;
        }

        int duplicate = 0;
        int missing = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            if (count[i] == 2) {
                duplicate = i;
            } else if (count[i] == 0) {
                missing = i;
            }
        }

        return new int[]{duplicate, missing};
    }
}
```

### Explanation

1. Create a `count` array to track how many times each number occurs.
2. Traverse `nums` and increment `count[num]`.
3. Iterate from `1` to `n`:
   - If `count[i] == 2`, the number is duplicated.
   - If `count[i] == 0`, the number is missing.
4. Return `{duplicate, missing}` in that order.

### Dry Run

Input: `nums = [1, 2, 2, 4]`

| Number | Frequency | Result        |
| ------ | --------- | ------------- |
| 1      | 1         | —             |
| 2      | 2         | Duplicate = 2 |
| 3      | 0         | Missing = 3   |
| 4      | 1         | —             |

Output: `[2, 3]`

### Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: \\(O(n)\\) — two traversals.
- Space Complexity: \\(O(n)\\) — the frequency array.

Interview tip: This frequency-array approach is easy to understand and efficient. You can also solve this problem in \\(O(n)\\) time using \\(O(1)\\) extra space by modifying the input array or using mathematical equations.

The easiest approach is to traverse the array and keep track of the minimum distance whenever we find the target.

### Java Solution

```

class Solution {
    public int getMinDistance(int[] nums, int target, int start) {
        int minDistance = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] == target) {
                int distance = Math.abs(i - start);
                minDistance = Math.min(minDistance, distance);
            }
        }

        return minDistance;
    }
}

```

